<title>Equilibria in discrete dynamical systems</title>

<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Video 2</ref> | <ref>Video 3</ref> | <ref>Transcripts</ref> </p>

<p>In 
<ref to="doenet:discrete_dynamical_system_introduction">discrete dynamical systems</ref>, there is a simple way to find equilibria.  Just plug a solution that does not depend on time into the evolution rule.  The result is an algebraic equation that you can solve to determine what the equilibrium solutions are.</p>

<p>For example, to find the equilibria for the discrete dynamical system
<me>x_{t+1} = 2x_t -1, </me>
substitute both <m>x_t=E</m> and <m>x_{t+1}=E</m>. Then, solve the equation <m>E = 2E-1</m> to determine that <m>E=1</m>.  The one equilibrium for this dynamical system is the solution <m>x_t=1</m>.</p>
  

